<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="article-commentary"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Australas. Chiropr. Osteopathy</journal-id><journal-title>Australasian Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathy</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1328-0384</issn></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">17987166</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2050814</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>INTEGRATIVE FASCIAL RELEASE &#x00026; FUNCTIONAL TESTING</article-title><subtitle>Commentary</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Hammer</surname><given-names>Warren</given-names></name><degrees>D.C., M.S.</degrees></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>3</month><year>2000</year></pub-date><volume>9</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>13</fpage><lpage>16</lpage><copyright-statement>Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathic College of Australasia</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2000</copyright-year><abstract><p>Soft tissue techniques, including Integrative Myofascial Release (IFR) can be more effective if the area of treatment can be determined by functional testing. The patient&#x02019;s source of pain may not necessarily be located at the area of complaint and functional testing helps in pinpointing the source. Post-treatment functional testing will provide feedback to both the patient and the doctor as to whether the technique was effective. This paper will describe some typical functional tests and treatment using IFR of the posterior cervical/thoracolumbar fascia.	  </p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Soft tissue</kwd><kwd>chiropractic</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>